  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Leopard Earthball (Scleroderma areolatum) is a small, round puffball fungus characterized by its distinctive leopard-spot pattern of areoles (polygonal markings) on its tan to brown surface. This species belongs to the Sclerodermataceae family and is commonly found throughout New England woodlands.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Areolate Surface Pattern",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is the network of raised, polygonal areoles that create a leopard-spot or cracked appearance on the peridium (outer layer)",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Shape",
      "description": "Small, spherical to slightly flattened fruiting bodies, typically 1-2 inches in diameter with a relatively smooth base",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Interior Color",
      "description": "When cut open, the gleba (spore-bearing tissue) is initially white or pale, becoming dark purplish-gray to black as it matures",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Leopard Earthballs fruiting from summer through fall in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests, typically appearing in small groups on the forest floor among leaf litter and mossy areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike true puffballs, Scleroderma species have thick, brittle peridiums that do not open with a simple pore, instead breaking irregularly to release their dusty spores—and these fungi are inedible and can cause gastrointestinal distress if consumed.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
